
--> Parameter
    @Parameter is used to document input parameters of an API.

    APIs often accept:
    Path parameters
    Query parameters
    Header values
    Optional filters
    @Parameter explains what those inputs mean, not just their data type.


    What It Enhances
    With @Parameter, Swagger can show:
    Parameter purpose
    Example values
    Whether it is required or optional
    Where it is passed (path, query, header)


    Why It Is Important
    Prevents misuse of APIs
    Reduces incorrect API calls
    Helps testers and frontend developers
    Improves API correctness and confidence
    In real projects, this avoids issues like:
    “What does this parameter actually do?”


